# portal 1.03 patch 27 — three failed fetches take the mode off
#
# The two modes have two documents, and one panel decides per request which of them to serve.
# That panel answers the engine's own format with `502` while the node list still arrives, so
# "the switch is on, so the subscription must be fine" was never true: the last good file stays
# on disk after a failed download on purpose (a working subscription is never replaced by an
# error page), which means a file that exists proves nothing about whether the panel still
# serves it. The owner saw a working list and a portal that could not be re-entered.
#
# Now both documents are counted, apart, and a mode is offered as long as its own count has not
# reached three failures in a row:
#
#   * `lib/core/portal_feed.dart` is the ledger: one counter per feed, process-wide and in
#     memory. `PortalFeed.engine` is the engine's format (`proxies:` + `rules:`, which the
#     network mode needs), `PortalFeed.xray` is the node list the simple portal runs.
#   * an attempt is counted where the fetch happens (`Profile._updateFrom`, and
#     `Profile._fetchXrayBody`): a `502`, an unparsable body, a client config with zero servers,
#     a share-link list that decodes to nothing and a request that throws all count the same.
#     The engine's attempt is counted on the save — the body parsed, the core accepted it, the
#     file was published — so a body that arrives and is not a config fails the count the way a
#     refused download does.
#   * each counter is reset by its own feed's next success and by nothing else. No restart is
#     needed: a success publishes the new count and the controls redraw.
#   * `portalAvailabilityProvider` merges the count with the document on disk (the stricter of
#     the two wins) and is what the controls read: the network mode's switch and the
#     split-tunnel filter strip go dead with a localized reason when the engine's format is
#     gone, the switch refuses the simple portal with its own reason when the node list is gone,
#     and both dead is one sentence naming both.
#   * `SetupAction.pickMode` refuses the mode (typed `unavailable`, so the switch reports it and
#     rolls back), and `_run` refuses to open a tunnel in a mode whose feed is gone — that is
#     what covers Quick Settings, Always-on and a restore, which never ask which mode to enter.
#   * every attempt is written beside the transitions, so a device check quotes the count:
#
#     kind=availability feed=engine mode=rule attempts=3/3 state=unavailable reason=http 502 · …
#     kind=availability feed=xray mode=global attempts=0/3 state=ok
#
# Strings: ru/en/zh_CN (`portalModeUnavailable`, `portalModesUnavailable`, `portalStrikes`).
# Version: 0.5.3-portal-duo-2c+2026111403, so this build is distinguishable from the 2b on the
# phones.
